%S A401687 2,3,1,2,0,3,0,0,0,0,28,0,6,1,0,0,31,29,27,1,23,7,5,1,0,0,38,32,34,32,
%T A401687 30,28,26,2,22,8,8,6,4,2,3,1,3,1,0,0,37,33,33,31,111,31,105,31,29,27,
%U A401687 29,27,25,23,25,23,21,9,9,7,5,3,14,4,12,2,8,4,10
%N A401687 a(n) is the number of steps required for the n-th prime to enter a loop when iterating the map: x -> nextprime(x*2) if primepi(x) is odd and x -> nextprime(x/2) otherwise.
%C A401687 It appears that all primes eventually fall into the same loop under iteration of the map. The loop contains 13 primes: {11, 23, 47, 97, 197, 397, 199, 101, 53, 29, 17, 37, 19}. Trajectories of prime(n) under iteration of the map for n up to 200 are shown in LINKS.
%H A401687 Ya-Ping Lu, <a href="/A401687/b401687.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A401687 Ya-Ping Lu, <a href="/A401687/a401687.pdf">Iteration trajectories of the first 200 primes</a>
%e A401687 a(1) = 2 because it takes 2 steps for the 1st prime to fall into the loop at 11: 2 -> 5 -> 11.
%e A401687 a(11) = 28 because it takes 28 steps for p(11) = 31 to fall into the loop at 47: 31 -> 67 -> 137 -> 277 -> 557 -> 281 -> 149 -> 307 -> 617 -> 1237 -> 2477 -> 4957 -> 9923 -> 19853 -> 9929 -> 4967 -> 2503 -> 1259 -> 2521 -> 5051 -> 2531 -> 1277 -> 641 -> 331 -> 673 -> 337 -> 173 -> 89 -> 47.
%t A401687 A401687step[x_] := A401687step[x] = NextPrime[x*If[OddQ[PrimePi[x]], 2, 1/2]];
%t A401687 A401687[n_] := First[FirstPosition[#, Last[#]]] - 1 & [NestWhileList[A401687step, Prime[n], UnsameQ, All]];
%t A401687 Array[A401687, 100] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Nov 10 2026 *)
%o A401687 (Python)
%o A401687 from sympy import prime, primepi, nextprime as np
%o A401687 def A401687(n):
%o A401687     D = {}; p = prime(n); s = 0
%o A401687     while p not in D: D[p] = s; s += 1; p = np(p/2) if primepi(p)%2 == 0 else np(p*2)
%o A401687     return D[p]
%o A401687 for n in range(1, 76): print(A401687(n), end = ', ')
